15. STOCK ACCOUNTING RULES AND RECONCILIATION
Maintain these stock buckets separately:

Coated Sheet Stock
Printing WIP
Approved Good Printing Stock
Rejected Printing Stock
Stock movement rules:

A. Coating Receiving: - Increase Coated Sheet Stock for the selected
item and Coating Batch Card Number.

B. Issue from Coating Stock to Printing Line: - Decrease Coated Sheet
Stock. - Increase Printing WIP for the corresponding Printing Batch Card
Number / issue reference.

C. Issue from Coating Stock to Crown Line, Plant 2 or Plant 3: -
Decrease Coated Sheet Stock. - Record a final coated-sheet factory
issue. - Do not increase Printing WIP.

D. Physical handover from Printing to Quality: - No software
transaction. - No stock movement. - Do not create Quality WIP.

E. Approved Quality Return: - Decrease Printing WIP by Good Quantity +
Rejected Quantity. - Increase Approved Good Printing Stock by Good
Quantity. - Increase Rejected Printing Stock by Rejected Quantity.

F. Final issue of approved printed sheets: - Decrease Approved Good
Printing Stock. - Record the destination factory.

Important: - Do not reduce Printing WIP when sheets are physically
handed to Quality. - Reduce Printing WIP only when the corresponding
Quality Return is approved. - Pending Quality Returns must not change
stock. - Rejected Stock must never be included in Approved Good Printing
Stock. - Do not allow negative stock. - Do not count the same sheets in
more than one stock bucket.

For every batch, calculate balances from posted/approved transactions.

Provide reconciliation checks so that: - Coating batch balance =
received quantity − all confirmed issues from that coating batch. -
Printing WIP balance = Printing Line issued quantity − approved Quality
Return quantity. - Approved Good Printing Stock balance = approved Good
quantity − final approved-printing-stock factory issues. - Rejected
Printing Stock balance = approved rejected quantity − any separately
authorized rejected-stock disposition, if such a process is implemented
later.

Do not invent a rejected-stock disposal workflow now. Keep rejected
stock separately visible and do not allow it to be issued as good stock.

If printing process loss or any other adjustment is required in future,
implement it only as a separately authorized, auditable transaction. Do
not silently subtract it from stock.

Show reconciliation warnings when balances do not match the underlying
transaction ledger.

